{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB06724 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Carbonate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving the formation of calcium salts. The evidence suggests that Calcium Carbonate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis, a condition characterized by the deposition of calcium salts in the kidneys, which in turn leads to the formation of other calcium salts such as Calcium Acetate and Calcium Glucoheptonate. These calcium salts are linked to Hypercalcemia Disease, indicating a chain of events where the accumulation of calcium in the renal system contributes to elevated serum calcium levels. The consistent association across three distinct paths highlights a potential mechanism by which Calcium Carbonate may exacerbate hypercalcemia, likely through the deposition of calcium in the kidneys and subsequent systemic effects. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of calcium salt formation in the development of hypercalcemia, emphasizing the importance of renal calcium homeostasis in this context.",
